  "side": "white",
  "summary": "1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.d4. You open the centre on move three and play for a lead in development. Two shapes cover almost everything you meet: the gambit with 4.Bc4, and the Classical with 4.Nxd4.",
  "coach_note": "You already win your Scotch games. The work is finishing them \u2014 taking the free piece, not checking into a hang, and never opening your king with f3 when Black's queen sits on e5.",
  "scheme": [
    "3.d4 opens the centre; decide on move four whether you want the gambit (Bc4) or the Classical (Nxd4).",
    "In the gambit, castle before any quiet pawn move. f3 with the king on g1 is a known self-mate pattern against ...Qe5.",
    "In the Classical, have a retreat ready when Black plays ...c5 (Qe3 or Qd3), then develop Be3/Bd2 and castle.",
    "Conversion: before you check, ask what hangs. Bb5+ and Bxf6 win more games than flashy Bxf7+ or Bc4+."
  ],
  "reading": [
    "Black's fourth move names the set-up: ...Bc5 and ...Nf6 lean gambit; ...Nxd4 and ...Qf6 lean Classical.",
    "If Black plays ...h6 early, take on d4 and develop \u2014 do not chase the queen into your own trap.",
    "A queen trade does not end the Scotch. Keep counting loose pieces."
  ],
